_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_

**String-based search may match comments or string literals.**

The `content.find(&search)` approach finds the **first** occurrence of `"{package_name} = {"` in the file, which could be inside a TOML comment or string literal:

```toml
[packages]
# mypackage = { old = "config" }  # <- matches here first
mypackage = { url = "https://..." }
```

This would incorrectly edit the comment instead of the actual package entry.



<details>
<summary>Suggested approach</summary>

Consider using `toml_edit` to insert the version key even for inline tables. While it may not preserve formatting perfectly, it would be correct. Alternatively, search only within the `[packages]` section boundary:

```diff
 fn add_version_to_inline_table(content: &str, package_name: &str, version: &str) -> Result<String> {
+    // Find the [packages] section to constrain search
+    let packages_start = content.find("[packages]")
+        .ok_or_else(|| ConfigError::Custom("No [packages] section found".into()))?;
+    let search_content = &content[packages_start..];
+    
     let search = format!("{} = {{", package_name);
-    let Some(brace_pos) = content.find(&search).map(|p| p + search.len() - 1) else {
+    let Some(relative_pos) = search_content.find(&search) else {
         // ... fallback handling
     };
+    let brace_pos = packages_start + relative_pos + search.len() - 1;
```

This reduces (but doesn't eliminate) the risk of matching comments before the `[packages]` section.

</details>

