_🩺 Stability & Availability_ | _🟠 Major_ | _⚡ Quick win_

<details>
<summary>🧩 Analysis chain</summary>

🌐 Web query:

`Rust sort_by panic user-provided comparison function does not correctly implement a total order`

💡 Result:

In Rust, the comparison function provided to sorting methods like `slice::sort_by` must implement a total order [1][2]. As of Rust 1.81, the standard library sorting implementations contain checks that may panic at runtime if they detect that the provided comparison function or the `Ord` implementation for the type does not satisfy the requirements of a total order [3]. A total order must satisfy three properties for all elements in the slice: 1. Totality: For any two elements `a` and `b`, either `a <= b` or `b <= a` must hold [1][2]. 2. Antisymmetry: If `a <= b` and `b <= a`, then `a` must equal `b` [1][2]. 3. Transitivity: If `a <= b` and `b <= c`, then `a <= c` must hold [1][2]. If these requirements are not met: - The function may panic [1][4]. - Even if the function completes without panicking, the resulting order of elements in the slice is unspecified [1][2]. This panic behavior was introduced to detect violations of the total order contract that were previously silent [3]. While it is a change in behavior, it does not alter the historical requirement that the comparator must define a total order; rather, it makes the enforcement of this precondition more rigorous [3]. If you encounter this panic, it indicates a logic error in your comparison function where it fails to maintain the necessary consistency and transitivity required for reliable sorting [5][4].

Citations:

- 1: https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/src/alloc/slice.rs.html
- 2: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/master/library/alloc/src/slice.rs
- 3: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/129561
